        <!-- ===== 01 · MYTH · PDB ===== -->
        <section id="myth" class="sec">
          <h2>Can You Really Tell a Hen From a Cock by Looking at an African Grey?</h2>
          <p class="opener">You cannot, and almost everything you will read today says otherwise. Search how to sex an African Grey parrot and the fourth result will tell you to lift the tail and read the feather colour — a method that has never worked on this species and has sent thousands of owners home with the wrong pronoun. We would rather spend the top of a sales page dismantling that than selling you anything.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "why-visual-sexing-african-grey-fails.webp"} srcset={IMG + "why-visual-sexing-african-grey-fails-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "why-visual-sexing-african-grey-fails.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="Why visual sexing an African Grey fails — tail feathers, eye shape and head colour are not sex markers" />

          <h3>The Tail-Feather Rule Everyone Repeats, and Why It Fails</h3>
          <p>The rule sounds testable, which is exactly why it spreads: red tail equals cock, grey or silvered tail equals hen. It survives because it is easy to remember and because nobody who repeats it ever gets contradicted — the bird cannot object, and most owners never run an assay to find out.</p>

          <h4>Do Red Tail Feathers Mean You Have a Cock?</h4>
          <p>No. A red tail means you have a Congo African Grey. Every healthy Congo carries one, hen and cock alike, and every Timneh carries a maroon one instead. The colour is a subspecies marker, not a sex marker, which is why the rule falls apart the moment somebody applies it to a room with more than one bird in it.</p>

          <h5>Silver Tipping, Eye Shape and Head Colour Are Not Sex Markers</h5>
          <p>The variants of the myth are more stubborn than the original. Silvered tips on the tail, almond eyes against round ones, a paler crown, a heavier beak — each of these is a real thing you can observe on a real grey, and none of them sorts hens from cocks. They vary with the individual, the moult and the light in the room.</p>

          <h6>From the Lab: Why Visual Cues Track Age and Wear, Not Sex</h6>
          <p>What those cues actually track is time. Tail tips silver as feathers wear and bleach before a moult replaces them; head shade lightens as a juvenile finishes maturing; eye rings change with age from grey through pale yellow. Read a bird's feathers and you are reading its calendar, not its chromosomes.</p>

          <h3>What Owners Discover After They Trust the Myth</h3>
          <p>The correction usually arrives from another owner. Ask a parrot forum whether the bird in the photograph is a hen and the answers arrive fast, unanimous and blunt: greys look identical, no physical feature determines sex, get a test or wait for an egg. It is good advice, freely given by people with nothing to sell.</p>

          <h4>Why the Question Keeps Coming Back Every Few Months</h4>
          <p>Because nothing in the buying process ever settled it. Someone buys a grey from a seller who never tested it, guesses at the sex from an article, names the bird accordingly, and then spends years quietly unsure. Our whole argument on this page is that the question should have been closed before money changed hands — by the breeder, at their expense, with the paper to show for it.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

        <!-- ===== 02 · MONOMORPHIC · EEBP ===== -->
        <section id="monomorphic" class="sec">
          <h2>What Does It Mean That <em>Psittacus erithacus</em> Is a Monomorphic Species?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Monomorphic is the technical word for the problem, and knowing it changes how you shop. It means the two sexes of a species grow into the same body — same size range, same colouring, same everything a photograph can capture. For <em>Psittacus erithacus</em> that is not a subtlety to squint past; it is the settled description of the bird, which is why a laboratory result is the only honest thing a seller can offer you.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "monomorphic-vs-dimorphic-parrot-species.webp"} srcset={IMG + "monomorphic-vs-dimorphic-parrot-species-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "monomorphic-vs-dimorphic-parrot-species.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="African Greys are monomorphic — cock and hen look identical, unlike a dimorphic species such as the Eclectus" />

          <h3>Monomorphic, Dimorphic and Where African Greys Sit</h3>
          <p>Split the parrot family by this one question and it falls neatly in two. Some species advertise sex in their feathers; the rest keep it to themselves. Greys are firmly in the second group, and no amount of familiarity with your own bird moves them into the first.</p>

          <h4>Which Parrots Can You Sex on Sight, and Which You Cannot</h4>
          <p>An experienced keeper can sex an Eclectus across a room, and will never manage it with a grey. That difference is not about skill — it is about whether the species evolved to signal sex visually at all. <a href="https://www.parrots.org/encyclopedia/grey-parrot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The World Parrot Trust's encyclopedia entry for the grey parrot ↗</a> is a useful outside reference here, because it describes the species without a bird to sell you.</p>

          <h5>Eclectus Parrots Are the Textbook Contrast to a Grey</h5>
          <p>Eclectus cocks are emerald green and hens are scarlet and blue, so plainly different that early naturalists catalogued them as two species. Set that beside two greys on a perch and the contrast makes the point better than any argument: one species answers the question for free, the other only answers it in a laboratory.</p>

          <h6>From the Lab: Why Colour-Coded Species Never Need an Assay</h6>
          <p>Genetics laboratories will happily run a sample from any bird, but nobody sends Eclectus feathers, because the plumage already carries the result. The demand for avian sexing assays comes almost entirely from the monomorphic species — greys, cockatoos, amazons, macaws — where feathers say nothing at all.</p>

          <img class="sec-img og-photo" src={IMG + "identical-cock-and-hen-congo-african-greys.webp"} srcset={IMG + "identical-cock-and-hen-congo-african-greys-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "identical-cock-and-hen-congo-african-greys.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="Two of our Congo African Greys photographed together, one cock and one hen, with no visible difference between them" />

          <h3>How Z and W Chromosomes Decide a Grey's Sex</h3>
          <p>Birds do not use the X and Y system people are taught at school. They run on Z and W, and the arrangement is inverted: a cock carries two Z chromosomes and a hen carries one Z and one W. A sexing assay is simply a test for the presence of that W.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "z-and-w-chromosomes-bird-sex-determination.webp"} srcset={IMG + "z-and-w-chromosomes-bird-sex-determination-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "z-and-w-chromosomes-bird-sex-determination.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="How Z and W chromosomes determine sex in African Grey parrots, with the hen carrying the deciding chromosome" />

          <h4>Why the Hen Carries the Deciding Chromosome</h4>
          <p>Since a cock can only ever pass on a Z, the hen's contribution decides which way each chick goes. It is a satisfying inversion of the mammal story, and it has a practical edge for us: because the W is a distinct sequence rather than an absence, the assay is looking for something positive to find rather than trying to prove a negative.</p>

          <img class="sec-img og-photo" src={IMG + "parent-african-greys-macy-letis-lineage.webp"} srcset={IMG + "parent-african-greys-macy-letis-lineage-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "parent-african-greys-macy-letis-lineage.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="Macy and Letis, the parent African Greys behind Jins and Jeni, photographed together in our Midland aviary" />
          <p class="cap">Macy and Letis, the parents behind Jins and Jeni. Every chick's sex was decided on Macy's side of that pairing.</p>
        </section>

