        <!-- ===== 03 · METHOD · EEBP ===== -->
        <section id="method" class="sec">
          <h2>How Does Avian DNA Sexing Actually Work on a Grey?</h2>
          <p class="opener">The process is far less dramatic than the word DNA suggests, and we think buyers deserve to see it laid out rather than gestured at. A small sample leaves the bird, travels to a genetics laboratory, gets amplified by PCR until the chromosomes can be read, and comes back as one word on a certificate. Four steps, roughly a fortnight door to door, and no anaesthetic anywhere in it.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "how-avian-dna-sexing-works-pcr-process.webp"} srcset={IMG + "how-avian-dna-sexing-works-pcr-process-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "how-avian-dna-sexing-works-pcr-process.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="The four steps of avian DNA sexing — sample collection, despatch to the laboratory, PCR analysis, result and certificate" />

          <h3>Feather Pulp or a Drop of Blood: Which Sample We Send</h3>
          <p><a href="https://avian2.animalgenetics.com/Avian/DNA_Sexing/DNA-Sexing-Index.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Avian DNA sexing services at Animal Genetics ↗</a> — the laboratory family we use, formerly trading as Avian Biotech — accept blood, feather and eggshell, and state that all three are equally reliable. That matters practically, because it means the choice of sample is about the bird in front of us on the day rather than about the quality of the answer.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "feather-versus-blood-dna-sample-parrot.webp"} srcset={IMG + "feather-versus-blood-dna-sample-parrot-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "feather-versus-blood-dna-sample-parrot.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="Comparing feather and blood samples for DNA sexing a grey parrot, and why we submit both types" />

          <h4>Why We Submit Both Sample Types</h4>
          <p>A settled young grey barely notices a few chest feathers coming away, so that is usually where we start. An older bird already having its toenails tipped gives us a blood drop with less fuss than a pluck would cause. Neither route is a compromise; we pick whichever is calmer for that individual, and the certificate records which one it was.</p>

          <h5>Blood Carries a Higher DNA Concentration Than Other Samples</h5>
          <p><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9913368/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peer-reviewed comparison of avian sample sources ↗</a> found molecular sexing from blood more accurate than from oral swabs, for the simple reason that blood carries more DNA per sample. Feather pulp sits comfortably between the two, which is why the pulp has to come with the feather — a shed feather off the cage floor has nothing left in it to read.</p>

          <h6>From the Lab: Blood Is Drawn From the Tip of a Toenail</h6>
          <p>The published method is a single drop taken from the very end of a toenail, not a syringe and not a vein. It is the same nick a slightly over-enthusiastic nail trim produces, and it stops within seconds.</p>

          <h3>What PCR Does With the Sample Once It Reaches Avian Biotech</h3>
          <p>Polymerase chain reaction copies a target stretch of DNA over and over until there is enough of it to see. Applied to a grey, the target is the chromosome pair — two Zs for a cock, a Z and a W for a hen — so the readout is a physical pattern rather than an interpretation. That is the whole reason we trust it over our own eyes after twelve years of looking at these birds.</p>

          <h4>How Long Do DNA Sexing Results Take to Come Back?</h4>
          <p><a href="https://avian2.animalgenetics.com/Avian/DNA_Sexing/DNA-Sexing-Feather.asp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Feather-sample turnaround times published by the laboratory ↗</a> put the analysis itself at one to two business days. Everything else in the fortnight is postage, which is worth knowing when a seller tells you results are "a few weeks away" — the assay is never the slow part.</p>

          <h5>Results Return in One to Two Business Days</h5>
          <p>That speed is precisely why we have no patience for a bird listed as tested before its result exists. Two working days is not a long wait to hold a page before publishing it.</p>

          <h6>From the Lab: Turnaround Counts From Receipt, Not From Posting</h6>
          <p>The published window starts when the sample is logged in at the laboratory. Add a few days each way for the post and you have the realistic timeline we quote buyers who ask how soon a newly hatched chick can be listed with its sex on the page.</p>

