                    <td data-label="Why it exists">{r.why}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h4>How Do You Match a Certificate to the Bird in Front of You?</h4>
          <p>Read the band, then read the paper, and make sure they agree. That is the entire verification, and it takes about fifteen seconds with the bird on your hand. A seller who cannot produce a certificate whose number matches the ring on the leg has given you a document about somebody else's parrot.</p>

          <h5>The Closed Leg Band Number Ties Paper to Parrot</h5>
          <p>A closed band goes on in the first weeks of life and cannot be slipped off an adult foot, so it stays a permanent identifier in a way a photograph never is. That permanence is what lets a piece of paper written months earlier still mean something on the day you collect the bird.</p>

          <h6>On the Record: What We Redact and What We Never Redact</h6>
          <p>When we send a certificate ahead of a purchase we cover our laboratory account number, because that identifies our billing rather than your bird. The band number, species, sample type, method, result and date always stay visible. If any seller blanks the band number, ask why — that is the one field a buyer actually needs.</p>

          <h3>How Accurate Is an Avian DNA Sexing Result?</h3>
          <p>Approximately 99 percent, which is the figure the laboratories publish about themselves and therefore the only one we will quote. It is high enough to plan a household around and honest enough to survive being checked.</p>

          <h4>Is Any Sexing Method Ever Truly Absolute?</h4>
          <p>Only an egg is, and eggs arrive on their own schedule. Everything before that is a very good instrument with a known error rate — which, for what it is worth, is a far better position than the confident guesswork it replaced.</p>

          <h5>Laboratories Publish Roughly 99 Percent Accuracy, Not 100</h5>
          <p>You will still see sellers advertise 99.9 percent. That figure appears nowhere in the published material of the laboratories doing the work, and a seller quoting it is either repeating something they never checked or improving on it deliberately. We would rather be the page that told you the real number.</p>

