        <!-- ===== 07 · ONE SAMPLE · EEBP ===== -->
        <section id="onesample" class="sec">
          <h2>Can One Feather Answer More Than the Sex Question?</h2>
          <p class="opener">It can, and this is the part of avian genetics no competitor on our SERP connects. The same feather pulp that reveals a Z and a W will also run the PCR panels for psittacine beak and feather disease and for avian polyomavirus. One pull from one bird, one envelope, and you learn who the grey is and whether it is carrying either of the two viruses that most often end badly.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "one-sample-sex-and-disease-screening.webp"} srcset={IMG + "one-sample-sex-and-disease-screening-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "one-sample-sex-and-disease-screening.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="One feather sample answers both the sex question and screens for beak and feather disease and avian polyomavirus" />

          <h3>Pairing Sexing With PBFD and Polyomavirus PCR Screening</h3>
          <p><a href="/african-greys-for-sale-with-health-guarantee/">Our health-guarantee page</a> sets out what we put in writing once those panels come back, so we will not repeat it here. What belongs on this page is the logistics: the disease screening is not a separate ordeal for the bird, because the material has already left it.</p>

          <h4>Why Sex and Disease Screening Belong in the Same Conversation</h4>
          <p>Because the awkward moment for a buyer is the same in both cases — asking a stranger to prove something about an animal you have not met. Asking once, about one sample, is easier than asking twice, and a seller who ran both is telling you something about how they operate. <a href="https://www.aav.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Association of Avian Veterinarians directory ↗</a> will find you an avian vet who can repeat either panel independently if you would like a second opinion.</p>

          <h5>Beak-and-Feather Disease and Polyomavirus Assays Run From the Same Draw</h5>
          <p>Both are PCR tests looking for viral DNA in the sample, which is why the same feather or blood draw serves all three questions. It is the single most practical thing we know about avian testing, and it is worth asking any breeder whether they used it.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

        <!-- ===== 08 · PENDING · PDB ===== -->
        <section id="pending" class="sec">
          <h2>Why Is a "DNA Pending" Listing a Reason to Wait?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Because it is an advertisement for a fact that does not exist yet. We have watched competitor accounts post a new chick with "DNA pending" underneath it, take enquiries all week, and never publish the result — and the honest reading of that is simple: the sale closed before the answer arrived, so the answer stopped mattering to anyone but the buyer.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "dna-pending-versus-result-in-hand.webp"} srcset={IMG + "dna-pending-versus-result-in-hand-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "dna-pending-versus-result-in-hand.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="The difference between a DNA pending listing, a presumed sex, and a bird with the laboratory result already in hand" />

          <h3>Pending, Presumed and "Breeder Says": Three Ways to Not Know</h3>
          <p>They shade into each other, and all three end with you owning a bird of unconfirmed sex. Pending means the sample is somewhere in the postal system. Presumed means somebody looked. "Breeder says" means somebody looked and was confident about it.</p>

          <h4>What Should You Ask a Seller Who Claims a Bird Is Sexed?</h4>
          <p>Four questions, and they take a minute: which laboratory ran it, what sample went in, what date the result came back, and what band number is printed on the certificate. Any breeder who actually tested the bird can answer all four from memory or from a folder. <a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Our scam guide</a> puts those questions alongside the other checks worth running before money moves.</p>

          <h5>Ask for the Certificate Before the Deposit, Never After</h5>
          <p>A deposit changes the conversation. Before it, you are a buyer choosing between sellers; after it, you are somebody hoping a document turns up. Ask while you still have the leverage.</p>

          <h6>On the Record: We List No Grey Until the Result Is Back</h6>
          <p>Two working days at the laboratory is not long enough to justify publishing a claim we cannot yet support, so we hold the page instead. It costs us a week of listing time per chick and we consider that a fair trade.</p>
        </section>

        <aside class="fs-nl banner" aria-label="Confirmed clutch alerts">
          <div class="fs-nl-inner">
            <p class="fs-nl-eyebrow">Confirmed-result list</p>
            <h3 class="fs-nl-h">Hear About Each Grey the Week Its Result Comes Back</h3>
            <p class="fs-nl-sub">One short email per bird the week its assay lands, carrying the confirmed sex and the price.</p>
            <form class="fs-nl-form" action="https://formspree.io/f/xrejpnvn" method="POST">
              <label class="vh" for="nl-email-dna">Email address</label>
              <input id="nl-email-dna" type="email" name="email" required placeholder="you@example.com" />
              <button class="btn btn-clay" type="submit">Send me confirmed results</button>
            </form>
            <p class="fs-nl-note">Result announcements only. Unsubscribe in one click.</p>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

        <!-- ===== 09 · FLOOR · PAS ===== -->
        <section id="floor" class="sec">
          <h2>When a Certificate Is Used to Make a Cheap Grey Look Legitimate</h2>
          <p class="opener">This is the uncomfortable half of the argument we have been making all page. A DNA certificate is a genuine trust signal, which is exactly why it gets bolted onto listings that deserve none — and right now a site ranking on page one for this very search is selling Congo greys at $750 under a "DNA Sexing Certificate Included" banner, with a struck-through $2,500 beside it.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "certificate-does-not-justify-sub-floor-price.webp"} srcset={IMG + "certificate-does-not-justify-sub-floor-price-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "certificate-does-not-justify-sub-floor-price.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="Why a DNA certificate badge on a sub-$1,500 African Grey listing is still a warning sign rather than reassurance" />

          <h3>The $750 Bird With a Trust Badge Attached</h3>
          <p>Look at that seller's own published price guide and it puts Congos at $2,500 to $3,200 — then discounts every bird on the site by roughly seventy percent at once, simultaneously, under a limited-time banner. Real aviaries do not clear their entire stock at half the cost of raising it, and a certificate badge does not change that arithmetic.</p>

          <h4>Why Does a Certificate Not Make a Sub-Floor Price Safe?</h4>
          <p>Because a certificate is a claim about one small fact and a price is a claim about everything else — incubation, months of feeding, veterinary care, paperwork, and whether the bird exists. A seller who wants to seem legitimate cheaply will always reach for the smallest verifiable-looking thing on the list.</p>

          <h5>Our Floor Stays at $1,500 Whatever the Paperwork Says</h5>
          <p>We have never placed a grey below it and we are not going to. If a listing pairs a certificate badge with a price under that floor, the certificate is the least reliable thing on the page.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

        <!-- ===== 10 · BEHAVIOUR · teaser, links out (cannibalization-capped) ===== -->
        <section id="behaviour" class="sec">
          <h2>Does Knowing the Sex Change How a Grey Behaves With You?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Less than most buyers hope. Once the certificate has settled the question, the honest follow-up is that it predicts fewer things about daily life than a good look at the individual bird does — and we would rather say so here than sell the result as more useful than it is.</p>
          <img class="sec-img og-photo" src={IMG + "dna-sexed-grey-with-owner-behaviour.webp"} srcset={IMG + "dna-sexed-grey-with-owner-behaviour-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "dna-sexed-grey-with-owner-behaviour.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="A DNA-sexed African Grey stepping up onto its owner's hand during daily household interaction" />

          <h3>What Sex Does and Does Not Predict</h3>
          <p>It predicts the possibility of eggs, which is worth planning for, and it settles what to call the bird. It does not reliably predict talking, temperament or how quickly a grey attaches to you. <a href="/male-vs-female-african-grey-parrots-for-sale/">Our male versus female comparison</a> takes that question apart properly, with the behavioural evidence weighed rather than summarised — this page's job was only to get you a trustworthy answer to start from.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

        <!-- ===== 11 · LEGAL · EEBP ===== -->
