          <h2>Is Owning a DNA-Sexed African Grey Legal Across the United States?</h2>
          <p class="opener">Yes, when the bird is captive-bred in the USA and its paperwork is in order — which describes every grey we place. The species sits on CITES Appendix I, and that listing governs international movement rather than whether an American family may keep one that hatched in Texas.</p>
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={IMG + "paperwork-travelling-with-a-dna-sexed-grey.webp"} srcset={IMG + "paperwork-travelling-with-a-dna-sexed-grey-760.webp 760w, " + IMG + "paperwork-travelling-with-a-dna-sexed-grey.webp 1408w"} sizes="(max-width:900px) 92vw, 760px" width="1408" height="768" loading="lazy" alt="The documentation travelling with every DNA-sexed African Grey, including CITES paperwork, closed band and health record" />

          <h3>CITES Appendix I, Captive-Bred, and Your Paperwork</h3>
          <p><a href="https://www.cites.org/eng/app/appendices.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The CITES appendices, where the grey parrot listing sits ↗</a> explain the uplisting that took effect in 2017. Nothing we sell has ever been wild-caught, and nothing crosses a border — our birds hatch here, fly domestically, and travel with the documents that prove it.</p>

          <h4>Which Documents Travel With a Sexed Bird</h4>
          <p><a href="/cites-african-grey-documentation/">Our documentation page</a> walks through each item in detail. On a sexed bird the folder gains one extra sheet — the certificate — which sits alongside the CITES paperwork, the closed band record, the avian vet health record and the care guide.</p>
        </section>

        <div class="seam-wrap" role="presentation"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" loading="lazy" width="182" height="60" /></div>

